The documented differences between the GT9895 I2C transfers of LilyGO's
RT-Smart driver (gt9895.c, golden_touch.c) and ours
(coproc/src/touch_k230.c) for one touch report. coproc/test/board_trace/
run.sh applies this to the vendor's log with `patch --fuzz=0` and requires
the result to be ours, byte for byte. Log lines: one I2C transfer each,
"w <bytes>" written, "r <n>" bytes read after a repeated start. Both write
the report address 00 01 03 08 (gt9895.c 99) and decode the same report
(the vendor's decoding of the model's bytes is checked by golden_touch.c,
ours by board_k230_test.c). Hunk 1:

  a. At open, the device check of LilyGO's Linux driver (0034-gt9895-touch
     .patch 1530-1560, 1490-1491): 8 x 0xAA written to 0x10000 and read
     back. The RT-Smart driver never checks (nor powers or resets the
     controller, which ours does first; GPIO, not I2C, so not in this log).
  b. The report read is 26 bytes, the header and two points with their
     checksum (Linux driver 2733-2735), more only for more than two fingers;
     the RT-Smart driver reads point_num x 9 = 90 bytes every time
     (gt9895.c 92, 195).
  c. After a report, 0x00 is written to its status byte (Linux driver
     2780-2790); the RT-Smart driver never clears it.

--- golden.trace
+++ ours.trace
@@ -1 +1,4 @@
-w 00 01 03 08 r 90
+w 00 01 00 00 aa aa aa aa aa aa aa aa
+w 00 01 00 00 r 8
+w 00 01 03 08 r 26
+w 00 01 03 08 00
